<!-- Instabridge vs Nomad Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set instaBest = providerA.codes | findCode("SP20") %}
  {% set nomadBest = providerB.codes | findCode("TRAVELESIMEXPERT") %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Instabridge</strong> if you mostly want free WiFi and only need a small, cheap data plan for the gaps. Pick <strong>Nomad</strong> if you want a dedicated travel eSIM app with a free 1GB trial, 24/7 live chat, and bundles that stay valid for up to a year. If price per GB on a single short trip is all that matters, check <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A WiFi App With an eSIM, or an eSIM App</h2>
  <p>
    Instabridge started as a WiFi-sharing app. Its own site advertises over 20 million free hotspots, and the eSIM is sold as a fallback for when free WiFi isn't around or isn't fast enough. Its plans are data-only, and on its US page they are small and short: 1GB for 7 days at $2, 3GB for 15 days at $4.50, 10GB for 30 days at $12, and 20GB for 7 days at $18 (as shown on Instabridge's web store this month).
  </p>
  <p>
    Nomad only sells eSIM data. Beyond its regular country, regional and global plans, it sells <strong>Global-EX</strong> bundles of 10GB to 50GB that stay valid for up to a year, which is a different product for someone abroad for months rather than days. The two are aimed at different readers: Instabridge at the light user who wants a cheap safety net, Nomad at the traveler who wants the eSIM to be the main connection.
  </p>

